WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:09.960
Amid this spring’s teacher walkouts, the
outlines of a sensible deal on teacher pay

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:10.960
seem pretty clear.

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:14.309
First, there’s a need for additional taxpayer
support.

00:00:14.309 --> 00:00:21.000
Terrific teachers are woefully underpaid and, in many states, average pay should be substantially higher.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.260
Second, an across the board increase isn’t
the whole answer.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:31.280
Some lousy teachers are currently overpaid,
even as exceptional teachers are wildly underpaid.

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:35.120
Current pay scales simply
don’t create room to use or appropriately

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:38.219
compensate teachers who are doing great work.

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:41.540
New taxpayer investment should help change
that reality.

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:46.680
Third, retirement benefits for educators 
cost twice as much as those for other workers.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:52.320
Teachers need to do their part, and agree to overhaul outmoded and expensive pension systems.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:56.580
Doing so will help free up substantial dollars for teacher take-home pay.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:01.220
Such a deal on taxes, pay systems, and pensions
involves short-term sacrifices.

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:07.360
But such a bargain has the potential to yield
big benefits for students, teachers, and taxpayers.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:12.340
Do you think we should raise teacher pay? Let us know in your comments.

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:17.480
Also, let us know what other topics you'd like our scholars to cover in 60 seconds,

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:23.840
and be sure to like and subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

